#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""Capture interactive Claude Code session transcripts into the Library's
Archives (issue_01M0BRGVMXGQKM4GXBEFG2Z7PS — the agent-platform half of
library#issue_01M0ASMWF82GD1849F2PK76PD5).

`~/.claude/projects` is a rolling ~30-day window (observed retention; see the
KB contract `agent-platform/contracts/claude-transcript-jsonl`). Nothing else
walks it, so a day not captured is a day of history gone for good. This job
walks it, groups each transcript's communication into turns, and POSTs each
turn to the Library as one Archives signal.

**Black-box exclusion is directory-name-only, and happens before this script
opens a single line inside an excluded directory.** `projects.private_slugs()`
names the excluded crews (today just `cfo`); for each, this forward-computes
the sanitized directory-name fragment its home directory, its `dirs`-registered
directories (ARCH-426), and its repos' worktree collections would produce, and
skips any `~/.claude/projects/<dir>` whose name contains one of them at a
'-'-delimited boundary. Recomputed fresh from the registry on every run —
never hardcoded to a slug, since ADR-0043 says more crews will follow.

**Capture is an allowlist: a directory is captured only when it resolves to a
registered, non-private project, and every other directory is skipped unopened,
exactly like a black box.** The black-box exclusion alone is a denylist, and a
denylist fails open for any session whose cwd no crew's fragment names. Two
did, until 2026-09-12. Ask's directory is not in the registry at all, so its
sessions were posted under their raw dirname. Headless `claude -p` jobs that
launchd starts with no WorkingDirectory run at `/`, whose dirname is `-`, and
that is how the EA crew's mailbox and calendar prompts reached the Library
despite EA being private. An unregistered directory now loses its archive
rather than leaking it; a job whose transcripts should be archived runs from a
registered directory instead of widening this.

**The match is a delimited substring anywhere in the dirname, not just a
leading prefix.** A worktree living under `<root>/worktrees/` or nested
inside a repo checkout (`<repo>/.claude/worktrees/<name>`) sanitizes to a
dirname that STARTS with the project's fragment, but a scratchpad cwd rooted
outside `<root>` (`/private/tmp/claude-<uid>/<origin-path>-scratchpad-
<name>`) embeds that same fragment partway through the dirname instead — a
leading-prefix-only check can never catch that shape no matter how many
fragments are enumerated. `dir_matches_prefixes` scans for the fragment at
any position, delimited by '-' (or a string edge) so that a private home_dir
of `.../CFO` cannot also swallow an unrelated `.../CFOextra` wherever it
appears.

**The match is case- and Unicode-normalization-insensitive, on purpose.** This
machine defaults to case-insensitive APFS, so a session whose cwd was reached
via different case (`<root-lowercased>/cfo` instead of `<root>/CFO`) sanitizes to a
differently-cased directory name that still names the SAME on-disk directory —
a naive case-sensitive prefix comparison misses it. `dir_matches_prefixes`
NFC-normalizes and casefolds both sides before comparing. This is a directory-
NAME comparison against a forward-computed prefix, not a live filesystem
resolution, so there is no symlink to chase on the transcript side; the
registry side already resolves through `projects.home_path()`, which follows
symlinks and refuses anything outside the home directory.

**Turn model**, reusing `lib/agent-platform/session_view.py`'s own
communication-first `classify()` rather than re-deriving the transcript
format: a turn is one `prompt`-kind FULL row plus every `speech`-kind FULL row
up to the next `prompt` row. Tool calls, results, and reasoning are COLLAPSED/
HIDDEN by `classify()` and never enter a turn. `classify_lines` additionally
dedupes repeated `assistant` records on `(message.id, requestId)` (ARCH-383):
Claude Code can write the same assistant response into the transcript more
than once, and without this a turn's body would repeat the same paragraph up
to 4x.

**Incremental, resumable capture.** A cursor file
(`~/.orient-control/transcript-capture.cursor.json`) maps each session file's
absolute path to a BYTE offset — the start of the earliest line not yet safely
captured. A turn not yet closed by a following prompt row (the session's most
recent turn, still possibly growing) is held back rather than posted with
partial content: only once the file has gone quiet for
`LIVE_THRESHOLD_SECONDS` is it treated as final and posted. The cursor never
advances past a turn that failed to POST, so a crash or a Library outage
re-covers that turn next run rather than losing it; `dedup_key` (a hash of the
session id and the turn's starting byte offset — stable across reruns since
the underlying file is append-only) makes a re-covered turn idempotent rather
than a duplicate.

Auth: a bearer token from `$TRANSCRIPT_CAPTURE_TOKEN` or the 0600 file
`~/.orient-control/transcript-capture-token` — this job's OWN `POST /signals`
credential (issue_01M3VY56TWPFFHQ50QTFM5P07K), separate from the shared
agent-platform one. `POST /signals` rate-limits each authenticated actor to
120/minute (library#176); this job alone can peak at 576/minute (see below),
which would otherwise hold the whole shared agent-platform bucket for minutes
and starve every other sender's signals. Until the Library mints and installs
this job's own credential, `capture_token()` falls back to the shared one
(`signal_client.token()`, the existing agent-kind `agent-platform` credential
in `api-tokens.json`, `products: ["*"]`, no `signal.create_customer` — this
job only ever writes `privacy_class: internal`, the server default) and warns
once to stderr. The actual POST is `signal_client.post_signal`
(ARCH-353/356), not a second implementation — this job can peak at 576
signals/minute (2026-09-16), well over the Library's 120/minute/submitter
ceiling (library#176), so it needs the 429/Retry-After retry `post_signal`
gives a caller that passes its own run `deadline` (see `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`
below), which this job does. Requests route through
`vault_plane`, which picks the live plane (the Library by default; see
`VAULTS_ON_LIBRARY` in `lib/agent-platform/vault_plane.py`) and attaches
`Library-Protocol: 1` when that plane is the Library.

A 429 still unresolved when this run's own deadline passes
(`_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`, from `main()`) comes back as `error:429`, counted in
`tally["errors"]` exactly like any other delivery failure — the file's cursor
does not advance past it (see capture_file's docstring), so the turn is
re-covered, not lost.

Modes:
  --backfill    every session file, ignoring the cursor
  (default)     incremental: only lines appended since each file's cursor
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import getpass
import json
import os
import sys
import time
import unicodedata
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

# Prefer this checkout's own lib over the installed farm (bin/knowledge-watcher's
# shape) — a Builder deploy area has bin/ and lib/agent-platform/ together but no
# ~/.local farm at all.
for _libdir in ((Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"),
                Path.home() / ".local" / "lib" / "agent-platform"):
    if (_libdir / "projects.py").is_file():
        sys.path.insert(0, str(_libdir))
        break

import projects  # noqa: E402
import session_view as SV  # noqa: E402
import signal_client as SC  # noqa: E402
import builder_root as BR  # noqa: E402

CLAUDE_PROJECTS = SV.CLAUDE_PROJECTS
CURSOR_FILE = Path.home() / ".orient-control" / "transcript-capture.cursor.json"
TOKEN_ENV = "TRANSCRIPT_CAPTURE_TOKEN"
TOKEN_FILE = Path.home() / ".orient-control" / "transcript-capture-token"

# How long a session file must sit unmodified before its trailing (possibly
# still-growing) turn is treated as final and posted. Matches the 10-minute
# OVERLAP convention the sibling captures (github_capture.py, gmail_capture.py)
# use for the analogous "don't act on a window that might still change" idea.
LIVE_THRESHOLD_SECONDS = 600

# The delivery-wanting budget (signal_client._DELIVERY_TIMEOUT's own reasoning
# applies verbatim: a one-shot launchd job cares that the write lands, not that
# it lands within the tee's 2s).
_POST_TIMEOUT = SC._DELIVERY_TIMEOUT
# No `attempts` is passed to signal_client.post_signal below — this job only
# wants the 429/Retry-After retry (via `deadline`), which post_signal gives
# independent of `attempts`. Passing attempts > 1 would additionally retry a
# 503/timeout/connection-error, a behaviour this job never had before this
# fix (its old hand-rolled POST made exactly one attempt) and does not need:
# a transcript turn that fails to post keeps its file's cursor from
# advancing (see capture_file's docstring), so it is re-covered next run
# either way.
# Bounds how long one capture run keeps resending a signal into the Library's
# POST /signals 429 (library#176, 120 signals/minute/submitter — see
# signal_client.post_signal's 429 handling). This job's own StartInterval is
# 900s (launchd/com.convivy.transcript-capture.plist); 600s leaves 300s of
# headroom before the next scheduled fire. A signal still unsent when this
# deadline passes comes back as 'error:429', counted in tally["errors"] like
# any other delivery failure — the cursor does not advance past it, so the
# turn is re-covered next run rather than lost.
_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS = 600

# <root>/worktrees — resolved once, the same collection dir
# lib/agent-platform/worktrees.py's `_extras_dir_for` computes, but defined
# independently here rather than imported: pulling in worktrees.py (which
# imports registry.py) for one path constant would widen this script's import
# surface for no benefit to a security-critical, directory-name-only check.
# The root comes from builder_root.root(), which is stdlib-only and reads no
# environment, so it widens nothing (company/decisions/0008).
_WORKTREES_ROOT = (BR.root() / "worktrees").resolve()

_MAX_IDENT = 64                    # library/store.py MAX_IDENT — POST /signals
                                    # 400s on source/kind/product over this
                                    # (actor's own cap is the separate, larger
                                    # store.MAX_ACTOR, 200 chars)
_MAX_CONTENT_BYTES = 20_000_000    # library/store.py MAX_BLOB_BYTES
_MAX_BODY_CHARS = 200_000          # library/store.py MAX_TEXT — POST /signals
                                    # 400s on body over this (library#201);
                                    # the check is `len(body) > MAX_TEXT` on
                                    # the Python str, i.e. characters, so the
                                    # clip below is a character slice, not a
                                    # byte slice


# --- black-box exclusion + product resolution (pure, unit-tested) --------------


def _sanitize(path: Path) -> str:
    """A directory Path -> the `~/.claude/projects` naming convention: every
    '/' AND every '.' becomes '-' (verified empirically against this
    machine's real `~/.claude/projects` entries, e.g. the on-disk worktree
    `agent-platform.black-box` sanitizes to
    `...-worktrees-agent-platform-black-box`, not
    `...-worktrees-agent-platform.black-box`)."""
    return str(path).replace("/", "-").replace(".", "-")


class _Prefix(NamedTuple):
    """One sanitized directory-name fragment a project's sessions can appear
    under, matched as a '-'-delimited SUBSTRING anywhere in a transcript
    dirname (see `dir_matches_prefixes`), not only at its start — a
    scratchpad cwd rooted outside <root> embeds a project's sanitized
    identifier partway through the dirname, not at the front of it.

    `boundary=True` means the match must be delimited by '-' (or a string
    edge) on BOTH sides — required for a home_dir/dirs_for fragment, where
    nothing else disambiguates "CFO" from "CFOextra" wherever in the string
    it appears. `boundary=False` means only the LEFT side needs delimiting —
    true for a worktree fragment, whose trailing '.' (from `<repo>.<purpose>`)
    is itself sanitized to a trailing '-' by `_sanitize`, so the fragment
    STRING already ends in the separator and its own last character already
    satisfies the right side (requiring one there too would look for a
    *second* '-' immediately after the first, which never occurs)."""
    value: str
    boundary: bool


def _project_prefixes(slug: str, unit: dict) -> list[_Prefix]:
    """Every sanitized directory-name prefix this project's sessions can
    appear under: its home directory, every other directory the registry's
    `dirs` field (ARCH-426) associates with it, and one per repo's worktree
    collection.

    `dirs_for(slug)` leads with `home_path(slug)` itself, so it is only
    consulted for the entries BEYOND home — a repo checkout that is not
    nested under the project's home directory (the shape `svc` already uses
    for `convivy-services`). Without this, a private crew registered with a
    non-empty `dirs` entry gets zero exclusion coverage for that directory
    tree, even though its own home directory is covered.

    Forward-computed from the registry, never decoded from a transcript
    directory name — that direction is ambiguous, since a repo name can
    itself contain '-' (`sous-chef`, `convivy-orient`), the same character the
    sanitizer uses as its separator.
    """
    out: list[_Prefix] = []
    home = projects.home_path(slug)
    if home is not None:
        out.append(_Prefix(_sanitize(home), boundary=True))
    for extra in projects.dirs_for(slug):
        if extra == home:
            continue
        out.append(_Prefix(_sanitize(extra), boundary=True))
    for repo in unit.get("repos") or []:
        if not isinstance(repo, str) or not repo:
            continue
        out.append(_Prefix(_sanitize(_WORKTREES_ROOT / f"{repo}."), boundary=False))
    return out


def all_project_prefixes() -> dict[str, list[_Prefix]]:
    """Every registered project's prefixes, {slug: [_Prefix, ...]}. Computed
    fresh from the registry each call — cheap (the registry load is cached,
    `home_path` is the only per-project syscall) and called once per run."""
    units = projects.load()
    return {slug: _project_prefixes(slug, unit) for slug, unit in units.items()}


def black_box_prefixes(project_index: dict[str, list[_Prefix]],
                        private: set[str]) -> list[_Prefix]:
    """The prefixes naming every black-box crew's sessions, drawn from the
    SAME `project_index` `resolve_product` uses — so exclusion and product
    resolution can never disagree about what a given prefix means."""
    out: list[_Prefix] = []
    for slug in sorted(private):
        out.extend(project_index.get(slug, []))
    return out


def _normalize(s: str) -> str:
    """NFC-normalize then casefold, so a comparison built on this is immune to
    both ASCII case (`CFO` vs `cfo`) and Unicode form differences — the two
    ways APFS's normalization-insensitive, case-insensitive default lets two
    different literal strings name the same on-disk path."""
    return unicodedata.normalize("NFC", s).casefold()


def _delimited_occurrence(dirname: str, fragment: str, *, require_right_boundary: bool) -> bool:
    """Whether `fragment` occurs in `dirname` at a position delimited by '-'
    (or a string edge) on the left, and — when `require_right_boundary` — on
    the right too.

    Scans every occurrence of `fragment` rather than stopping at the first:
    an early occurrence can fail the boundary check (e.g. `fragment`
    reappearing, unboundaried, as the tail of some longer unrelated token)
    while a later, genuinely-delimited occurrence exists further along the
    same string.
    """
    start = 0
    flen = len(fragment)
    while True:
        i = dirname.find(fragment, start)
        if i == -1:
            return False
        left_ok = i == 0 or dirname[i - 1] == "-"
        right_ok = (not require_right_boundary
                    or i + flen == len(dirname)
                    or dirname[i + flen] == "-")
        if left_ok and right_ok:
            return True
        start = i + 1


def dir_matches_prefixes(dirname: str, prefixes: list[_Prefix]) -> bool:
    """Whether a `~/.claude/projects/<dirname>` belongs to one of `prefixes`.

    Case- and normalization-insensitive throughout (see `_normalize`). Each
    prefix is matched as a delimited SUBSTRING anywhere in `dirname`, not
    only at its start (`_delimited_occurrence`) — required because a
    scratchpad cwd rooted outside <root>
    (`/private/tmp/claude-<uid>/<origin-path>-scratchpad-<name>`) embeds a
    project's sanitized identifier partway through the dirname, e.g.
    `-private-tmp-claude-501--Users-operator-Convivy-CFO-somehash-
    scratchpad-probe`: the private home_dir fragment `-Users-operator-
    Convivy-CFO` sits after `claude-501-`, never at the front, so a plain
    `startswith` can never catch it no matter how many prefixes are
    enumerated (security-reviewer, PR #415, run-3a454557). A `boundary=True`
    prefix requires the delimiter on both sides, so a private home_dir of
    `.../CFO` does not also swallow an unrelated `.../CFOextra` wherever in
    the string it appears; a `boundary=False` (worktree) prefix already
    carries its own trailing '-' (sanitized from `<repo>.`), which is itself
    the right-side delimiter, so only the left side is checked.
    """
    norm_dir = _normalize(dirname)
    for p in prefixes:
        norm_p = _normalize(p.value)
        if _delimited_occurrence(norm_dir, norm_p, require_right_boundary=p.boundary):
            return True
    return False


def resolve_product(dirname: str, project_index: dict[str, list[_Prefix]],
                     private: set[str]) -> str | None:
    """The registered, non-private project slug a transcript directory belongs
    to, or None when none matches, and None means `main()` skips the directory
    unopened.

    This used to fall back to the raw directory name, and that fallback is the
    leak the module docstring describes: every unregistered directory,
    including `-` and ask's, was captured under its own name.

    `private` is excluded from the candidate pool as a second, independent
    guard alongside the caller's own exclusion check — belt-and-suspenders,
    matching `library/identity.py`'s own pattern of a capability check backed
    by an unconditional kind check on the write route: a bug in the caller's
    exclusion pass must not be able to smuggle a black-box crew's slug into a
    signal's `product` field via this path instead.
    """
    for slug in sorted(project_index):
        if slug in private:
            continue
        if dir_matches_prefixes(dirname, project_index[slug]):
            return slug
    return None


# --- turn extraction (pure, unit-tested) ----------------------------------------


def classify_lines(lines: list[str], offsets: list[int]) -> list[dict]:
    """Parse and classify each transcript line, keeping only FULL prompt/
    speech rows — the human-visible communication `session_view.classify()`
    already separates from tool calls, reasoning, and control-plane noise.

    Degrades per record rather than raising: an unparseable line, a non-dict
    JSON value, or a `classify()` failure is skipped and the rest of the file
    still processes — matching `classify()`'s own documented "never raises"
    contract and the KB contract's warning that one malformed line must not
    take a whole transcript's capture down.

    Deduplicates repeated `assistant` records (ARCH-383): Claude Code can
    write the identical assistant response into the transcript more than
    once. A record counts as a repeat only when BOTH `message.id` and
    `requestId` are present and match one already seen in THIS call; a record
    missing either key is never collapsed with another keyless one, matching
    the contract's own dedupe rule for cost consumers.
    """
    seen_assistant: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
    out: list[dict] = []
    for line_idx, raw in enumerate(lines):
        text = raw.strip()
        if not text:
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(text)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — degrade per record, never crash the file
            continue
        if not isinstance(rec, dict):
            continue
        if rec.get("type") == "assistant":
            message = rec.get("message")
            msg_id = message.get("id") if isinstance(message, dict) else None
            req_id = rec.get("requestId")
            if isinstance(msg_id, str) and isinstance(req_id, str):
                dedup = (msg_id, req_id)
                if dedup in seen_assistant:
                    continue
                seen_assistant.add(dedup)
        try:
            rows = SV.classify(rec)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — classify() documents "never raises";
            continue        # degrade defensively anyway rather than trust that blindly
        ts = rec.get("timestamp") if isinstance(rec.get("timestamp"), str) else None
        byte_offset = offsets[line_idx] if line_idx < len(offsets) else None
        for row in rows:
            if row.get("display") != SV.FULL:
                continue
            if row.get("kind") not in ("prompt", "speech"):
                continue
            out.append({**row, "_byte": byte_offset, "_timestamp": ts})
    return out


def group_turns(rows: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:
    """FULL prompt/speech rows -> turns: `{"start_byte": int, "rows": [...]}`.

    A turn is one `prompt` row plus every `speech` row up to the next
    `prompt` row. Rows opening with `speech` and no `prompt` seen yet in this
    read window (the first lines read from a file whose earlier history
    predates this window) start a turn anchored on that first row rather than
    being dropped — losing them silently would be worse than an occasional
    turn with no human line at its head.
    """
    turns: list[dict] = []
    current: dict | None = None
    for row in rows:
        if row.get("kind") == "prompt":
            if current is not None:
                turns.append(current)
            current = {"start_byte": row.get("_byte"), "rows": [row]}
        else:
            if current is None:
                current = {"start_byte": row.get("_byte"), "rows": [row]}
            else:
                current["rows"].append(row)
    if current is not None:
        turns.append(current)
    return turns


def _turn_text(rows: list[dict]) -> str:
    parts = []
    for row in rows:
        body = (row.get("body") or "").strip()
        if not body:
            continue
        label = row.get("label") or row.get("kind") or "?"
        parts.append(f"{label}: {body}")
    return "\n\n".join(parts)


def turn_payload(session_id: str, product: str, actor: str, turn: dict) -> dict | None:
    """One turn -> a `POST /signals` payload, or None for a turn with no
    visible text (e.g. every row's body was blank).

    **`body` and `content` derive from one sanitized string, but `body` is
    then clipped to `_MAX_BODY_CHARS` while `content` keeps the full text.**
    A transcript line can carry a lone Unicode surrogate (`\\ud800`) — valid
    JSON, not valid UTF-8. The library's `POST /signals` refuses an
    unencodable `content` with a 400 (library/app.py, PR #40), but has no
    equivalent guard on `body`, which reaches SQLite's INSERT and raises
    uncaught. Since `capture_file` only advances a session's cursor on a
    zero-error run, one such turn would wedge that session's cursor
    permanently, silently dropping everything appended after it. The
    encode/decode roundtrip below sanitizes before truncating to
    `_MAX_CONTENT_BYTES`, and the final decode with "replace" absorbs any
    multi-byte sequence the truncation itself splits — so the one resulting
    string is always safe to store as `content`. `POST /signals` separately
    refuses a `body` over `_MAX_BODY_CHARS` characters (library#201) rather
    than truncating it the way it used to, so `body` takes a further
    character-count clip of that same sanitized string; `content` is the
    blob column (`_MAX_CONTENT_BYTES`, 20 MB) and is unaffected."""
    text = _turn_text(turn["rows"])
    if not text:
        return None
    content_bytes = text.encode("utf-8", "replace")
    if len(content_bytes) > _MAX_CONTENT_BYTES:
        content_bytes = content_bytes[:_MAX_CONTENT_BYTES]
    sanitized = content_bytes.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    body = sanitized[:_MAX_BODY_CHARS]
    produced_at = next((r["_timestamp"] for r in turn["rows"] if r.get("_timestamp")), "")
    return {
        "source": "claude-transcript",
        "kind": "transcript-turn",
        "actor": actor,
        "product": product[:_MAX_IDENT],
        "stream": session_id,
        "body": body,
        "content": sanitized,
        "dedup_key": f"claude-transcript:{session_id}:{turn['start_byte']}",
        "produced_at": produced_at,
    }


# --- I/O: transcript reads, signal POSTs, the cursor, the token ------------------


def _read_new_lines(path: Path, start_byte: int) -> tuple[list[str], list[int], int]:
    """Complete (newline-terminated) lines appended after `start_byte`, each
    one's starting byte offset, and the byte offset just past the last
    complete line. A trailing partial line (the file mid-write) is excluded
    and never counted into that offset, so a later run rereads it once it is
    whole rather than missing the completed record forever."""
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        fh.seek(start_byte)
        raw = fh.read()
    lines: list[str] = []
    offsets: list[int] = []
    pos = 0
    end_of_complete = start_byte
    while True:
        nl = raw.find(b"\n", pos)
        if nl == -1:
            break
        offsets.append(start_byte + pos)
        lines.append(raw[pos:nl].decode("utf-8", "replace"))
        pos = nl + 1
        end_of_complete = start_byte + pos
    return lines, offsets, end_of_complete


def capture_token() -> str:
    """This job's own `POST /signals` credential (`TOKEN_ENV` or `TOKEN_FILE`),
    else the shared agent-platform one (`signal_client.token()`) with a
    one-line stderr warning — see the module docstring's Auth section. Exits
    only when NEITHER credential is available; a missing own-credential file
    alone must not stop the run, since the fallback keeps it posting through
    the switch to a dedicated actor."""
    tok = os.environ.get(TOKEN_ENV, "").strip()
    if tok:
        return tok
    try:
        file_tok = TOKEN_FILE.read_text().strip()
        if file_tok:
            return file_tok
    except OSError:
        pass
    print(f"transcript-capture: no {TOKEN_FILE} and no ${TOKEN_ENV}; sharing the "
          "agent-platform signal bucket until the Library mints "
          "transcript-capture's own credential", file=sys.stderr)
    shared = SC.token()
    if shared:
        return shared
    sys.exit(f"no capture token: set {TOKEN_ENV}, create {TOKEN_FILE}, or set "
             f"{SC.TOKEN_ENV}/create {SC.TOKEN_FILE}")


def post_signal(payload: dict, token: str, *, deadline: float | None = None,
                sleep=time.sleep) -> str:
    """POST one signal. Returns 'created' | 'duplicate' | 'error:<code>'.

    Through `signal_client.post_signal` (ARCH-353/356) rather than a second,
    independent POST implementation — see this module's own docstring for why
    that matters here (a 429 from the Library's rate limit, library#176).
    `deadline` (a `time.monotonic()` value) bounds how long a 429 is retried;
    `main()` passes this run's own budget through a `poster` closure built
    from this function rather than changing `capture_file`'s `poster(payload,
    token)` contract, which every existing caller (including every test
    `poster=` lambda) still relies on. `sleep` is the test seam for that
    retry's wait — tests override it so a 429/Retry-After loop does not
    consume real wall-clock time; production never passes it, so it is always
    the real `time.sleep`.

    No `attempts` is passed through — see `_POST_TIMEOUT`'s neighbouring
    comment for why this job wants only the 429 retry `deadline` already
    gives it, not the 503/timeout retry `attempts > 1` would add."""
    return SC.post_signal(payload, tok=token, timeout=_POST_TIMEOUT,
                          deadline=deadline, sleep=sleep)


def load_cursor() -> dict:
    try:
        return json.loads(CURSOR_FILE.read_text())
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — a missing/corrupt cursor just means "start fresh"
        return {}


def save_cursor(cur: dict) -> None:
    CURSOR_FILE.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    CURSOR_FILE.write_text(json.dumps(cur, indent=1))


def _actor() -> str:
    try:
        return getpass.getuser() or "unknown"
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return "unknown"


# --- the capture run -------------------------------------------------------------


def capture_file(path: Path, start_byte: int, now: float, product: str, actor: str,
                  token: str, poster=post_signal) -> tuple[int, dict]:
    """Capture new turns from one transcript file. Returns (new_cursor_value,
    tally). The returned cursor value is meaningful only when
    `tally["errors"] == 0` — `main()` persists it only in that case, so a
    file with any POST failure keeps its old cursor and re-covers the same
    window next run (dedup_key absorbs the safe re-posts)."""
    tally = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "skipped": 0, "errors": 0}
    session_id = path.stem
    try:
        stat = path.stat()
    except OSError:
        tally["errors"] += 1
        return start_byte, tally
    # A cursor past the current file size means the file was rotated/replaced
    # out from under us; restart from the beginning rather than seeking past EOF.
    read_from = start_byte if start_byte <= stat.st_size else 0
    try:
        lines, offsets, end_of_complete = _read_new_lines(path, read_from)
    except OSError:
        tally["errors"] += 1
        return start_byte, tally
    if not lines:
        return read_from, tally
    rows = classify_lines(lines, offsets)
    turns = group_turns(rows)
    if not turns:
        return end_of_complete, tally
    closed, trailing = turns[:-1], turns[-1]
    to_post = list(closed)
    if now - stat.st_mtime >= LIVE_THRESHOLD_SECONDS:
        # The file has gone quiet: the trailing turn is not going to grow any
        # further, so it is safe to treat as final.
        to_post.append(trailing)
        new_cursor = end_of_complete
    else:
        # Still live: hold the trailing turn back and reread it (plus
        # whatever gets appended to it) next run rather than posting it now
        # and losing anything appended to it after this post — the Library
        # never updates a row's content on a dedup_key collision.
        new_cursor = trailing["start_byte"]
    for turn in to_post:
        payload = turn_payload(session_id, product, actor, turn)
        if payload is None:
            tally["skipped"] += 1
            continue
        result = poster(payload, token)
        if result == "created":
            tally["created"] += 1
        elif result == "duplicate":
            tally["duplicate"] += 1
        else:
            tally["errors"] += 1
    return new_cursor, tally


def main() -> None:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Capture interactive Claude Code session transcripts into "
                     "the Library's Archives")
    ap.add_argument("--backfill", action="store_true",
                    help="ignore the cursor and reprocess every session from the start")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if not CLAUDE_PROJECTS.is_dir():
        print(f"{CLAUDE_PROJECTS} does not exist; nothing to capture")
        return

    token = capture_token()
    actor = _actor()
    project_index = all_project_prefixes()
    black_box = black_box_prefixes(project_index, projects.private_slugs())
    cursor = {} if args.backfill else load_cursor()
    now = time.time()
    # One deadline for the whole run, not per signal — see _RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS.
    deadline = time.monotonic() + _RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS
    poster = lambda payload, tok: post_signal(payload, tok, deadline=deadline)

    grand = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "skipped": 0, "errors": 0}
    excluded = 0
    unregistered = 0
    for dirpath in sorted(p for p in CLAUDE_PROJECTS.iterdir() if p.is_dir()):
        if dir_matches_prefixes(dirpath.name, black_box):
            # Excluded at the directory level: nothing inside it is opened,
            # not even to list its files.
            excluded += 1
            continue
        product = resolve_product(dirpath.name, project_index, projects.private_slugs())
        if product is None:
            # No registered public project owns it: skipped just as unopened.
            # `-` (cwd `/`) and ask's dir both land here.
            unregistered += 1
            continue
        for f in sorted(dirpath.glob("*.jsonl")):
            key = str(f)
            start = 0 if args.backfill else cursor.get(key, 0)
            new_cursor, tally = capture_file(f, start, now, product, actor, token,
                                             poster=poster)
            for k in grand:
                grand[k] += tally[k]
            if tally["errors"] == 0:
                cursor[key] = new_cursor
    save_cursor(cursor)
    print(f"excluded {excluded} black-box directories")
    print(f"skipped {unregistered} unregistered directories")
    print(f"total: {grand}")
    if grand["errors"]:
        sys.exit(1)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
